menu "LagunBus — epd"

config LAGUN_TWAI_TX
    int "TWAI transmit GPIO"
    default 4
    help
        4 and 5 by default, because on the ESP32-C3 Super Mini they are broken out
        and play no other role. AVOID 2, 8 and 9 — those are boot-mode strapping
        pins, and their state at reset decides whether the board starts at all.
        Avoid 18 and 19 too: USB Serial/JTAG goes that way, that is, flashing and
        the log.

config LAGUN_TWAI_RX
    int "TWAI receive GPIO"
    default 5

config LAGUN_I2C_SDA
    int "I2C SDA GPIO (e-paper)"
    default 6
    help
        6 and 7 by default: broken out on the Super Mini, on the same side as the CAN
        RX, and no strapping role. The Waveshare module carries its own pull-ups.

config LAGUN_I2C_SCL
    int "I2C SCL GPIO (e-paper)"
    default 7

config LAGUN_EPD_RST_GPIO
    int "e-paper RST GPIO"
    default 10

config LAGUN_EPD_BUSY_GPIO
    int "e-paper BUSY GPIO"
    default 20
    help
        20 by default (zadania/zrobione/DEVICE-04.md, N2): UART0 RX, free as an input
        because the console goes over USB Serial/JTAG — and with it every pin of the
        screen is on the right side of the board. The ROM sends on GPIO21 only.

config LAGUN_EPD_MIN_GAP_S
    int "Seconds between refreshes, at least"
    default 60
    range 1 3600
    help
        A change of a number waits at most this long for the glass (DEVICE-04, N3).
        Not while the glass shows no number: a value after a start or an expiry goes
        out at once. The maker asks for minutes between refreshes — every one wears
        the panel; set it low only for a bring-up.

config LAGUN_EPD_FULL_EVERY
    int "Partial refreshes before a full one"
    default 10
    range 1 50
    help
        A partial refresh leaves a trace of the old picture; a full one (the whole
        glass flashes) wipes it (DEVICE-04, N3).

config LAGUN_EPD_TEST_PATTERN
    bool "Test pattern instead of the node"
    default n
    help
        For the first power-up of a panel (DEVICE-04, step 2): no node, no bus — the
        board steps through patterns every few seconds and says in the log what
        each one should show: every byte alone, the bits of bytes 12 and 13, every
        digit in every place, the minus and the dashes. Compare the glass with the
        log; the segment map in components/epd1in9 comes from code, not from the
        maker's notes.

config LAGUN_SERVICE_GPIO
    int "Service button GPIO"
    default 9
    help
        The service button: held 5 s and let go, a factory reset by the device itself
        (devices/README.md). 9 by default — BOOT on the Super Mini, wired to ground; an
        external button goes from GPIO9 to GND next to it. Held at power-on it enters
        the download mode instead, which harms nothing: let go and power up again.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_GPIO
    int "Status LED GPIO"
    default 8
    help
        The status LED: what the node is doing (devices/README.md). 8 by default — the
        on-board LED of the Super Mini; an external one goes from 3V3 through a resistor
        to GPIO8. GPIO8 is a strapping pin and must not be pulled to ground at reset.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "Status LED lights on a LOW level"
    default y
    help
        Yes for the Super Mini's on-board LED and for an external one wired from 3V3.

config LAGUN_BITRATE
    int "Bus speed [bit/s]"
    default 500000
    help
        Has to agree with the hub and with EVERY other node. A node at a different
        speed not only hears nobody — it corrupts frames for all the others.

endmenu
